Setup:
I am using a Samsung 2GB MLC flash with 4K page size and 8-bit
hardware ECC support. I am using a 2.6.31 kernel, but if I read the=20
code right, the problem I see is in the latest 2.6.34-rc2 code as well.=20

Summary:
I traced a torture test caught in a repeating loop, wearing out a=20
flash block. It appears that the torture test with it's simple=20
0xA5/0x5A/0x00 pattern is passing, whereas actual application data=20
is consistently showing a bit flip on the same block. MLC flashes I=20
have used regularly show corrected errors over several blocks in time,=20
and also show corrected errors consistently due to program-disturb=20
effects, but these program disturb effects appear to be data dependent,=20
based on device geometry (e.g. adjacent multi-bit cell effects). The=20
scenario above can cause an repeating scrubbing / torture test loop=20
to occur. It is initiated by the nand driver reporting a bit correction=20
in a block which starts the scrubbing/torturing loop.=20

Details:

(Please correct me if I have got ubi parts of this explanation wrong - Than=
ks).

A single bit correction is a relatively normal event with MLC flash and=20
this triggers a scrub operation in UBI in which the block is considered=20
marginal, and the data is moved from a marginal block to a good block. A=20
good block is one that is considered to have passed the UBI torture test,=20
which simply writes 0xA5, 0x5A, and 0x00 to a block with 3 erases, and=20
then does a 4th erase for the user data that will eventually be copied to=20
this block. But this torture test is too simplistic since it doesn't=20
understand the geometry of MLC flashes and in particular, write-disturb=20
and read-disturb effects from neighboring cells and columns. What's maybe=20
needed is a torture test that understands the geometry and the bit correcti=
on=20
level 1,4,8,12 etc and is able to toggle geometrically neighboring bits rat=
her=20
than view it as a simple memory test, but this may be difficult. Perhaps=20
torturing MLC blocks with only 3000 cycles is inappropriate anyways, why no=
t=20
just trust the error correction and only mark uncorrectable blocks as bad?.=
=20
Since the torture test passes with no bit errors I believe that it didn't=20
find certain error correction patterns that appear with actual application=
=20
data. As a result, when the torture test passes, the block shows no correct=
able=20
errors, and is returned to the free pool. The original block X that had a=20
correctable error is scrubbed and it's data is copied to the free block wit=
h=20
the highest erase count. The highest erase count block is chosen and will r=
emain=20
partially written and available for new scrubs, and data will be scrubbed t=
o it=20
from other marginal blocks while the other blocks catch up in erase count. =
However,=20
if block Y previously had a bit error and was torture tested and passed, it=
's=20
erase count will go up by 4. As a result, block Y has a higher probability =
of=20
being the target of the copy from X to Y. But when the copy of actual appli=
cation=20
data is done, block Y now shows a data-dependent correctable bit error agai=
n and=20
the scrub operation fails, and block Y is again sent out for torture testin=
g, and=20
the process repeats itself until block Y either shows no error or the block=
 wears=20
out, is marked bad, and another free block is chosen. So not only do blocks=
 wear=20
out, but many blocks can be taken out of service as well by being marked ba=
d. If=20
the available pool of free blocks for bad block management (typically 1% of=
 the=20
total blocks in a partition) go bad, then the file system is remounted read=
-only=20
and the product becomes unusable for writable data in that partition. If I =
have a=20
3000 erase MLC part, then only 750 quick loops of the scrub/torture (4 eras=
e) cycle=20
will wear the block out. At that point the next free block will be used whi=
ch may=20
or may not show a corrected error.

Possible Solutions:
I think that with MLC flashes, we perhaps shouldn't be so aggressive to scr=
ub/torture=20
given the more frequent error rate than we see with SLC parts. Also hiding =
the actual=20
corrections in the nand driver to hide the problem in UBI isn't a good long=
 term=20
solution easier. The UBI algorithm doesn't appear to understand different l=
evels=20
of correction possible. For example, on a 12-bit part, an single corrected =
error=20
is much more common and normal than say, 11 bit corrections on a marginal b=
lock,=20
but UBI treats 1 or 11 bits the same algorithmically. The ecc correction ca=
pability=20
maybe should be known by UBI so it can make better decisions about when to =
run=20
scrub/torture algorithms? Or perhaps we don't do torturing at all with MLC =
flash and=20
rely on the multi-bit correction instead to help us. The correction level a=
nd the=20
flash type could be useful information for UBI to use in deciding algorithm=
s like these.

The short term solution might be for the nand driver to hide error correcti=
ons from=20
the UBI wear leveling software by reporting all good corrections as 0 error=
s. Then=20
UBI shouldn't start scrubbing and torturing on a single bit error in a bloc=
k. Two=20
blocks with a single error that are part of the scrubbing/torturing duo sta=
rt the=20
loop of flash erasing/wear out.=20

I also attach a log file I collected demonstrating the problem, with some a=
nnotations=20
inline. The log are basically the existing printk's plus a few of my own co=
nverted to=20
log quickly to RAM to avoid cluttering the console and destabilizing real t=
ime.=20

I hope this helps us understand and support MLC flash a bit better. I am cu=
rious to=20
know if anyone else has seen problems like these and how they dealt with th=
em.

Thanks.
Darwin

